course: dictionary
lesson: 13900
title: Describing people with lanky
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Writers use describing words to help you picture a person. Lanky is one of them. Look at the clues around it: «The lanky teenager ducked under the low door.» He had to duck, so he must be tall. Lanky tells you he is tall and thin too.
explain: Lanky has a second meaning, for hair. «Her lanky hair hung straight past her ears.» Hair cannot be tall, so here lanky means long, straight and limp, with no bounce in it.
explain: Be kind when you use lanky. It describes how someone looks, and some people may feel teased by it. Use it to paint a picture in a story, not to hurt a friend.
fact: Lanky is an adjective, so it describes a noun, such as a person, a leg or some hair.
fact: Lanky means tall and thin in an awkward, long-legged way.
fact: Lanky can also mean long, straight and limp, when talking about hair.
fact: The comparative is lankier and the superlative is lankiest: «He is lankier than his brother.»
fact: Lanky can sound unkind, so think about how the person might feel before you use it.
exercise: «The lanky teenager ducked under the low door.» What does lanky describe? | A loud noise | *A tall, thin person with long legs | A bright red room | A small, round dog
exercise: «Her lanky hair hung straight past her ears.» What does lanky mean for hair? | Bright and shiny | Curly and short | *Long, straight and limp
exercise: «His lanky legs stuck out from the bed.» Find and copy the word that describes his legs. | out | *lanky | stuck | bed
test: Which word means nearly the same as lanky when describing a person? | Loud and cheerful | *Tall and thin | Short and round | Old and slow
test: What is the comparative form of lanky, as in «the lankier boy»? | lankest | *lankier | lankiest | lanky
exam: Jayden says lanky means fat. Is he right? | No, it means very loud | Yes, it means short and wide | Yes, it means round and soft | *No, lanky means tall and thin
